Phil Mendelsohn wrote:
> This may be more clear when you tell what group you were with and
> what service you provided.

Having observed Barbara's participation on alt.folklore.computers and
alt.sys.pdp10, I believe she was in, and possibly later managing,
a group origianlly called tape duplication services, later turning
into Software Distribution Center for 36-bit systems. And she had
a close personal relationship with some or all of the senior TOPS-10
developers, but was never a programmer herself.
 
On Fri, 14 Sep 2001 jmfbahciv@aol.com wrote:
> > People didn't generally type in their programs on-line (there was no
> > such thing in Cutler's area...only the PDP-10 provided on-line
> > computing service in those days). These people used coding sheets,
> > submitted them to us to transform into a specified flavor of bits, and
> > went back to their office to write more code on _paper_.

We are talking early 1970's here. If this was how things were done
at DEC, then it would have been a technological backwater.

1) Lots of PDP-11 software developemnt (including all hardware 
   diagnostics) was indeed done on TOPS-10, which had a cross-
   assembler and linker system.
2) RSX-11D had a resonable Time-Sharing system running on PDP-11,
   as did RSTS-11.

Either of these systems would have allowed online editing of source
code, feeding into a version control system. Just because Barbara
ran "the data entry shop" used by lots of departments, including
RSX, and she did not see such submissions, it does not mean that
such work did not take place.

Most of the work I did from 1975 to 1980 was related to kernel
add-ons to RSX-11M: Device drivers and real-time applications
industrial data acquisition, factory control, and data communi-
cations. I was very impressed with RSX-11M, and I can confirm
that the module headers in EVERY kernel module listed D.C. as
the original author, and about 25% of later modifications in
the core modules also carried his initials. Barbara essentially
claims that his name is there only because he was the manager
for the group; I find that hard to believe. I am more inclined
to believe that he was an extremely gifted system programmer,
and an obsessively detail oriented control freak. I can believe
that he would not have been fun to work for, but the work that
came out of that group was of the highest quality.

I can also believe that he was a key influence on the design of
the VAX/VMS architecture, whcih was indeed a close co-design of
hardware and software.

On the other hand, I have also observed VAX machines running on
VMS and VAX machines running on 4.3BSD, and the BSD machines
delivered much better user performance on the same iron horsepower.
I am willing to believe that TOPS-10 was essentially as quick and
nimble as BSD. But this has to be tempered with the understanding
that VMS provided - from day one - a lot of code in the RMS
(Record Management Subsystem) for which there is no equivalent in
Unix or TOPS, and which haas immense value for the business
data processing that was the reason for the existence of VAX/VMS.

The PDP-11 market was fractured into about a dozen different
operating systems, which made it hard for DEC to manage. Essentially,
there were several product lines for hardware and each operating 
system was its own product line. This was good for customers, 
because there were separate product lines each with their own DEC 
sales teams *and* VARs competing for the customer's business, so
all of them were very responsive. But it was bad for DEC, because
the product lines would beat each other up. Management was deter-
mined not to repeat that, so there was a religious adherence to
VMS as the one true system for the VAX. Only after 4.3BSD had
established a very strong foothold, did DEC create a UNIX product
line: Originally called Ultrix, then Digital Unix, and now Tru-64
or some such.

DEC fumbled both the personal computer and workstation markets
in inter-divisional squabbles. Just as Robin, Rainbow and PRO-11
could each have spawned a viable PC prodict line, so could a
workstation business have been built on either MicroVAX/Unix
or a cost-reduced DEC-10 architecture (Mars-like). It didn't
happen, and it is riduculous for 32-bitters and 36-bitters to keep
shooting at each other while the current (or should I say recent)
management is pissing away the 64-bit business. Which only goes
to show that it was not an issue of evil individualt, but of a
dysfunctional senior corporate management culture.
