>
> Thanks in advance.
> -Swarna.
>
What version of Tru64?
You can use uerf -R -r 300 should show the boot record from the
last system boot, although I have heard of problems on V5 systems
with uerf (you can use dia instead).
Other messages seen during boot come from applications started in the boot
process and you should be able to modify the scripts which start them to log
output to a file as well.
Another way which we have used to good effect is to use a serial console and
grab the output in the terminal emulator.
Hope this helps
Adam




