> Rumor has it that -11M was the core.  He may have done a good
> piece of that code before I started working there, 1971.

Cutler had written RSX-11A and brought it with him from DuPont.  He
approved by hand every line of privileged code in 11M.  That was after '73
because that's when our family moved to MA.  11M was probably a little
later, because Dad was somewhere else in DEC for a little while before he
moved in the Mill to Central Engineering.  (Life Sciences Marketing, I
think -- very different than what Marketing is known for today -- they
spent more time explaining to engineering how they needed this or that
kind of I/O for A/D because of customer needs, etc...).

My Dad wrote the error logging database subsystem for -11M, and had to
negotiate with Cutler to justify the use of 2 _bits_ in a database.  I've
talked with him about Cutler and VMS quite a bit, but doubt he'd be
willing to spend much time on usenet to talk about it himself.  Dad was
also on the VAX "B" design team, but I don't know as much about that
(except that they had two "opposing" redundant design teams -- A would
come up with a plan and submit it to B, who would tear it apart.  
Anything that was finally left standing got in.) But, he was always very
proud that the error logging was one of the big pieces of 11M that went
over to VMS.  (Relatively untouched.)

Cutler most certainly was a programmer.  At one time, it was very
difficult to code faster or smaller or more robustly than he could.

Little stories like his Red Rubber Stamp for the -11M project (designed to
be -11D in less memory) that said "Size *IS* Important" which was likely
to be put on code that he could write smaller before returning it to the
author indicate that some did consider his management "style" abrasive.  
But the VMS kernel was his brainchild.

I'm not a fanatic Cutler-worshipper (or hater), but this belittling of his
role is a puzzler, and is easy to prove wrong, if you talk to the people
who were actually there, and if you keep engineering history separate from
the history of Orifice Politics.  God only know what happened to any of
the documents from the project -- they may be in VMS Engineering's
equivalent of the storage room at the end of "Indiana Jones".

> > ...and was a pivotal force in managing that
> >release's successful creation 
> 
> This may be true.  But that would have been the first
> version and not subsequent versions.

You'd probably know better than I.  This doesn't explain much about what
he did at DEC between '76 and '88 or '89 when he left.  I sense a lot of
personal opinion in this.

> > ..(even though his management style could take a toll on others).
> 
> <grin>  I've dealt with him.  

He's an alpha-male who's usually quick to get several correct answers and
decide which one he likes -- that's my spin.  Hrm... do people dislike
that because it makes him too different from them, or too much the same?
;)

-- 
I liked HP before computers, and at one time I liked Compaq,
but I liked DEC better than HP and Compaq put together.




