In comp.sys.hp.hpux Mikko Nahkola <mnahkola@trein.ntc.nokia.com> wrote:
>>Note that HP is close behind Sun (IMHO) -- they offer patches individually
>>and as part of a coherent bundle called an ACE (Additional Core
>>Enhancement(s)).

> HP also offers coherent patch bundles called "HWCR" (hardware-related & 
> critical), "GR" (general release), and "QPK" (quality package) ... and 
> some others. 

  HWCR are simply patches to support new hardware such as peripherals
  and new I/O cards as well as fixes to hardware-specific drivers
  and support commands.

> But I thought that an ACE would rather contain new functionality and 
> possibly new hardware drivers than bug fixes... was I wrong?

  Sort of.  ACE is one of many acronyms for a new Install CD.  The
  various dates for ACE CDs relate to the date they are needed to
  support new processor models.  ACE CDs are primarily for workstations.

  For 800-series, the equivalent CDs were called HWE (HardWare Enablement)
  but both ACE and HWE naming conventions have been abandoned.  They
  were used for 10.20 (now quite mature and due for retirement next year).
  For 11.0, the latest Core/Install/Recovery CD is July 2001 and supports
  the latest processor models.  This is why old CDs from 3-4 years ago
  won't work on new models like the N-4400 or A-550, etc.  

> I believe that the "QPK" patches are tested eve more than the rest of the 
> bundles. Or that's what said in a related brochure once...

  SupportPlus is the common name for patch and diagnostic collections.
  It is issued quarterly and is also downloadable from the ITRC.

  QPK is the workstation version of GR (General Release). On the SupportPlus
  CD, the individual depots are XSW (Extension SoftWare) so the GR bundle
  is XSWGR1100 and hardware is XSWHWCR1100.  Unfortunately, the naming
  convention is changing again...for 11i (aka, 11.11) the names are now
  changed to GOLDAPPS11i and HWEnable11i.

>>2) HP-UX has better (although *way* more convoluted to use) tools for
>>patch maintenance.

  Correct.  The level of control and protection is extensive.  Software
  Distributor (for 11.00 and higher) has exceptional controls on patch
  installation, all related to dependencies and prerequisites.  Unix
  has gotten very complex so a simple patch is no longer simple.

>>c) There's a tool to clean out old patches backups, that are no longer
>>needed.

> Um. Isn't that something you'd do anyway? I find the whole concept quite 
> interesting... not being able to clean out unused old versions if you 
> want to, that is. It is quite another issue to not be able to _keep_ the 
> old versions (as in some other operating systems)... but that's why we 
> have backup tapes, right?

  Actually, the reason is much more complex and has to do with patch
  interdependencies.  There are patches that depend on other patches to
  be correctly installed.  Removing one patch may break other functions
  that depend on that one patch...thus the need to actively manage 
  patches.  At 11.00 and higher, you can commit a patch (and it's
  relatives) so the old files can be removed.  This means the patch
  cannot be removed.

