Hi Rolf,

Rolf Wester wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> 
> I have a question concerning core dumps. I have a program written in c++
> (compiler is cxx) that makes a segmentation fault with subsequent core
> dump. This happens some time after program start, so debugging would be
> quite tedious. Is it possible to get information about the source line
> where the segmentation fault happens from the core dump?

typically: Yes. 
"Debugging" does not necessarily mean "tracing" or "single-stepping". 

You should start by getting a stack backtrace from the core dump, 
the command is specific to the debugger you use: 
"adb" -> '$c',   "dbx"/"gdb" -> 'where',   "sdb" -> 't'. 
This should give you a function name, where the segmentation fault 
(= use of an invalid address via a pointer) occurred.

If that info is insufficient, add a "debug" option ('-g' ?) 
and recompile the program. When degugging the new core, 
you should also see line numbers.

Regards, 
Joerg Bruehe

