In article <qPcm7.250377$J37.64427127@typhoon.southeast.rr.com>,
Ken Farmer <kfarmer@tru64.org> wrote:
> Don't stop there...what else do you like about HP-UX?  Anything else you
> think is better or worse?

I like lots of stuff about both, my response to Jack was just to defend
HP-UX's filesystem offerings, which are top-notch (Doug Siebert's post
about HP phasing out their LVM (IBM's LVM) in favor of Veritas' really
sucks though, IMHO, for all the reasons I mentioned in my previous post
about IBM's LVM versus VxVM).

Here's an area where HP-UX kicks Tru64's butt: The patch process.

Long-time admins of Tru64 know that it used to be that you had to apply
and track patches *manually*.  After lots of outcries from admins that
knew that other platforms (Solaris, HP-UX, heck even AIX!) did it better,
they came out with `dupatch' and associated tools.  The patch process
got much better, but it still lags a long ways behind platforms like
Solaris.

My reasoning:

1) Compaq only releases patches in giant, "coherent" bundles.  You can
apply patches individually from the bundle, but you have to fetch the
bundle as one giant glob.  Worse, the giant glob is only released once
the entire glob passes SQA.  This means that a patch you're waiting for
to a particular command or subsystem could be held up for *months* while
some completely unrelated portion of the patch glob goes through SQA.
If you beg nicely, they'll often give you an unofficial patch, but it's
always a situation where you're back to applying, tracking, and then
later (when the official version becomes available) backing out the patch
manually.

Contrast this with a company like Sun, that releases patches in discrete
packages (one patch contains the minimum bits necessary to fix a particular
command or subsystem).  If you only need a fix for the `ls' command, you
only have to download that, and you don't have to wait months and months
while some unrelated patch makes it through the SQA process.

Even better, Sun *also* releases patches in two different forms of
coherent bundles -- the "Recommended & Security" bundle and the "Maintenance
Update" (MU) bundle.  These big bundles are tested together (like
Compaq's giant glob) and are therefore less likely to have weird interrelated
problems, but unlike Compaq you don't have to wait for the giant glob if
you don't want to.

Note that HP is close behind Sun (IMHO) -- they offer patches individually
and as part of a coherent bundle called an ACE (Additional Core
Enhancement(s)).

2) HP-UX has better (although *way* more convoluted to use) tools for
patch maintenance.

a) The tools to install patches are the exact same tools that are used to
install any other fileset.  There's no tool distinction between installing
a patch and installing a core fileset, as there is with Tru64 (you
use `dupatch' for installing patches, but you use `setld' for loading other
types of filesets).

b) Even more important, with HP-UX you can actually *verify* a package
that has been patched.  With Tru64, the `fverify' command doesn't realize
when a patch has been installed, thereby changing the checksum of one
or more binaries, so it will flag any base fileset that has been patched
as having inconsistencies, even though the inconsistencies come about
because of a patch!

c) There's a tool to clean out old patches backups, that are no longer
needed.

I do have to say that HP-UX's software packaging tools are going to give
beginning HP-UX managers (and Tru64 crossover managers) fits.  The whole
"depot" concept and how to actually be able to install a peice of software
is complex.  It takes a while to get a handle on.


If I think of other areas, I'll post more, but that's just one that
jumped out at me right away.

Tim
