In article <2de05464.0109100737.32a56054@posting.google.com>,
Newbie JrSysAdmin <utlonghornsrule@yahoo.com> wrote:

> good time to ask a question: where (in /etc/system?) do you limit the
> number of active processes? if it's just available memory versus the
> process table,

No, that's a Solaris-only thing.

> then, obviously, there's no practical limit.

Tru64's equivalent of Solaris' /etc/system would be /etc/sysconfigtab.
You'll want to read the man pages for sysconfig, sysconfigtab, dxkerneltuner,
and especially sys_attrs and the sys_attrs subgroup manpages.

Note that like most UNIX systems with a strong BSD history, Tru64 uses
a parameter called `maxusers' as a scaling factor for a bunch of different
kernel tuneables.  If you tune maxusers up, a lot of other settings scale
up automatically.  Pay special attention to that section of the sys_attrs_proc
manpage.

If you want to tune *just* the maximum # of processes any one non-root user
can have, you could tune `max_proc_per_user'.

