Sept. 6, 2001, 6:23PM

Sadly, Hewlett-Packard going 'Way' of dot-coms 
By MICHAEL S. MALONE 

For more than six decades, Hewlett-Packard has played a special role
in the life of Silicon Valley.

It has always been the antipode, the counterpoint, to the Valley's
excesses. In a town of wildcatters and lone wolves, HP was the home of
Little League coaches and den mothers. Good companies emulated HP; bad
companies were shamed by it. And when things went bad -- your company
died or you grew tired -- HP was sanctuary, where you could rest,
retune your skills, unlearn your selfishness, make a real
contribution, then charge back out into battle.

Hewlett-Packard has also been a social contract unique in American
industry. It says to employees: Live up to the tenets of the HP Way
and the company in turn will give you a fulfilling lifetime of work.

Thousands of employees have done just that, some into the second
generation. But that was before Carly Fiorina.

Under her tenure, HP has been changing before our eyes into something
we know all too well. It is becoming just another Silicon Valley
company: massive layoffs, living from quarter to quarter, serving
stock analysts rather than customers, emphasizing pricing and market
share rather than innovation and profits.

"We just have to trust that Carly knows what she's doing," one 20-year
employee told me recently. Then he shook his head. "She's supposed to
be really smart, so there must be a plan there."

But now, with this week's announcement of a buyout of Compaq, even the
most loyal HPer must be asking: Does Carly Fiorina have a clue what
she's doing with Hewlett-Packard? Does she understand that this is the
jewel of American industry she is toying with as if it is just some
resume entry?

The answer is: No. She didn't understand the company when she got
there, and she doesn't understand it now. And she will soon kill the
greatest company ever known.

Harsh words, but they pale against the magnitude of what is going to
be lost. Fiorina's dwindling population of supporters -- computer
retailers and the inexcusably stupid HP board of directors -- say that
she has no choice, the world has changed and HP has to change with it.
That these extraordinarily difficult times require radical measures.

Oh? The economy was far worse in 1974, when HP chose to cut everyone's
salary by 10 percent rather than undergo layoffs. That was radical.
When HP metamorphosed from an instrument company to a computing giant
-- that was real change.

But the biggest myth of all is that the HP Way is somehow obsolete. In
fact, it is never more competitive than right now.

The company Bill Hewlett and Dave Packard built was dedicated to
producing the most innovative, highest quality products possible for
targeted markets that needed them. For these products, customers would
pay premium prices and still feel they'd gotten a bargain. From this
simple model, everything else followed. You only stayed in markets
where the model worked, and abandoned those (especially the ones
becoming commoditized) where it didn't. You took the long view in all
of your business planning, assuming that stock prices always followed
success. And most of all, you trusted and retained your employees,
because only they fully understood both the technology and the
customer.

The power of the HP Way has only been underscored by the dot-com
debacle, in which thousands of would-be Bills and Daves ignored
everything but the stock

options and the Friday afternoon beer busts. A little more dedication
to real products and real profits would have changed history.

Apparently they didn't learn the HP Way at Lucent either. Why else
would Fiorina throw away thousands of years of HP intellectual
capital, chase a dying, commodity market like PCs and make every
decision as if she's got one eye cocked on CNBC? Why else would she
abandon the one business philosophy most other companies wish they had
the guts to try?

And now the Compaq deal. Fiorina has decided to bet Hewlett-Packard on
a fading industry that her predecessors would have long since
abandoned. And she is doing so by making HP the final link in a daisy
chain of failure.

Gloomy, dying DEC bets the ranch on a new computer chip and fails. It,
in turn, is bought by creepy, dysfunctional Compaq, which fails to
effectively absorb it. And now Hewlett-Packard buys this unhealthy,
inassimilable mess in order to gain economies of scale in an industry
where scale ultimately doesn't matter.

It will fail. The market knows it. HPers know it. 

At least Fiorina will now find herself surrounded by people who don't
believe in the HP Way either. Together they can price-cut and lay off
the merged companies into oblivion.

Still, mindful of the role HP has played in the history of Silicon
Valley and the electronics age, Carly Fiorina can perform one last
service to the memory of William Hewlett and David Packard.

She can name the new company 

Compaq.


