> ISTR IA64 is little-endian.

IA64 is bi-endian, as are Alpha and Power (don't know about SPARC).  But
HP-UX is big-endian, and Tru64 is little-endian, and never the twain shall
meet (at least not without significant non-transparent pain to at least one
of them).

>
> And if you think Tru64 has given away most of its differentiating goodies,
> well, you haven't been paying attention.

I suspect you're the one who hasn't been paying attention.  Compaq's
open-source project is in the process of porting single-system-image
clustering facilities to Linux, for example.

> Where's Linux clustering?

Linux clustering is everywhere, though admittedly not yet as mature as
Tru64's.  A group of Tru64 developers formed Mission Critical Linux over a
year ago and have a product out.  Other products exist for Web service (no,
they're not full-function clusters IMO, but they do that job well).  I'm
hardly an expert on the overall state of clustering on Linux, but know that
HPC cluster facilities like Beowulf have recently been joined by several
high-availability clustering approaches, with more on the way.

 Beowulf?
> And either of AdvFS or UFS are better than Ext2fs.

And XFS is probably better than any of them - and was released on Linux last
May.  AIX's JFS is also a respectable filesystem, and was released on Linux
recently.  I like a lot of what's in Reiserfs too, but AFAIK it's not as
ready for commercial use yet.  Ext3fs may or may not yet be product-quality,
but should eliminate most objections to Ext2fs in an upward-compatible
manner.

- bill

