"JF Mezei" <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote in message
news:3B9AC8F5.50E57870@videotron.ca...
> Peter da Silva wrote:
>
> > ISTR IA64 is little-endian.
>
> OK, reality check time:
>
> Does it really matter if an OS is big or little endian ?

In the context of this discussion, beyond any shadow of a doubt.

The context is the 'convergence' of Tru64 and HP-UX.  The former is
little-endian, the latter big-endian.  That means:

1)  Forget about transparently mingling Tru64 nodes and HP-UX nodes in the
same cluster:

a)  At the lowest level, the same application binaries won't run on
different-endian nodes - *even if both nodes are Alphas, or both nodes are
Itanics* - since Tru64 binaries were complied to run on little-endian
hardware and HP-UX binaries were compiled to run on big-endian hardware.

b)  Text-only files could be shared by both platforms (assuming they adopted
common cluster protocols - itself a mammoth undertaking), but no file
containing integer data wider than a single byte could, because data
structures in files are not self-describing hence even if you knew in
which-endian mode the file had been created you wouldn't know - except
within the application, using application code explicitly written for this
purpose - the structure of the data and which data elements required
conversion.  If the cluster supports truly-shared devices (or even single
ownership with fail-over), only same-endian nodes can handle any given
device unless the file structure (meta-data) on that device is changed to be
endian-independent (or at least the file system code is changed to be
endian-sensitive).

2)  Forget about moving application source code transparently between
platforms of different endianness (even if they're both running the same
OS), unless you've verified that the code is in no way endian-sensitive.
Since all DEC platforms have been little-endian since before DEC had a Unix
at all (unless Ultrix ran big-endian on MIPS, 'way back when?), expect a
fair amount of endian-sensitive code to be out there.  (Code ported from
Windows environments, all of which are little-endian, is often
endian-sensitive as well.)

3)  Forget about moving data transparently between platforms of different
endianness (even if they're both running the same OS), unless you've
verified that it's not endian-sensitive.

 I realise that you
> need to know in order to convert data you receive from other machines, but
> other than that, does it matter ?

Pretty much only if you want to mingle mixed-endian platforms in the same
cluster, or port code that is endian-sensitive, or share data that is
endian-sensitive.  In the context of this discussion, all are likely
important issues.

>
> And at the OS level, would it be a huge task to recompile Tru64 to run on
> Alpha in big endian mode ?
> (eg: are there a LOT of assumptions in the OS about the endianness of the
> platform ? or are they handled by the compiler/cpu  ?)

That's not a relevant question unless you've already ascertained that all
applications and non-byte-structured data of any real significance could be
moved to a big-endian environment without problems (which is not to say
without effort).  And of course you still couldn't mix big-endian Tru64
nodes with little-endian Tru64 nodes in the same cluster, or move
non-byte-structured data transparently between nodes of different
endianness.


So IMO the idea that Tru64 and HP-UX will 'converge' is poppycock.  At best,
HP-UX will inherit some useful features from Tru64.  But Tru64 itself is
toast, for the reasons I gave previously (plus the fact that HP-UX has 9%
penetration in the enterprise space compared with 2% for Tru64, last I
heard):

"Tru64 suffers in this acquisition from being the technology of the acquiree
rather than the acquirer, from competing with an existing Unix owned by the
acquirer, from being a little-endian system that won't easily be melded with
the acquirer's big-endian system, from being currently available only on a
lame-duck hardware platform, and from already having given a lot of its
differentiating goodies to Linux."

- bill





