> other than that, does it matter ?
> 
> And at the OS level, would it be a huge task to recompile Tru64 to run on
> Alpha in big endian mode ?
> (eg: are there a LOT of assumptions in the OS about the endianness of the
> platform ? or are they handled by the compiler/cpu  ?)
>

Dunno much about C and Unix, but from the VAX calling standard point of view:

When passing integers to subroutines by reference (the default in VMS), the 
endianness is all important. If you want to pass an Integer:4 to a routine 
expecting an Integer:2 or Integer:1, it works fine because the least 
significant byte is in position 0 (which is the address passed).

Indeed, COBOL doesn't understand Integer:1, so the only sensible way is to 
use an Integer:2 (PIC S9(4) COMP in COBOLese) when an Integer:1 is expected 
in the called routine.

Changing endianness (is that a real word?) would create an awful lot of work 
in converting such programs. Of course, this is speaking from a VMS angle.
___
Paul Sture
Switzerland



