PMFJI, but this same discussion regarding big-endian versus
little-endian w.r.t. the IA64 took place in the comp.sys.tandem group
back when Compaq announced it would use that chip for future Himalaya
platforms.

It turns out that, while the IA64 instruction processing, per se, is
little-endian, an OS can set the chip's "data mode" to big-endian if
desired.  Meaning that, for all intents and purposes for application
programming, a transition to the IA64 is no more severe than a
transition from a CISC chip to a endian-compatible RISC chip.
```
