>
> Forget about transparently mingling HP-UX and Windows nodes in the same
> cluster, too. Microsoft may well be the ten ton gorilla that forces HP-UX
> to make an endian flip.

Possibly.  But another option is to create a common file system that can
handle mixed endianness, assume that applications that themselves need to
run mixed-endian will be written handle their own internal data properly
(possibly by making it endian-neutral, which can be inefficient but
eliminates the need for endian-sensitive code), and define a heterogeneous
node community that's somewhat less tightly-coupled than the current
definition of a cluster.

There are a lot of situations in which this would address needs at least as
well as current clustering does (at the cost of somewhat messier management,
unless new software were created to mask the heterogeneity), and it would
have the advantage of allowing current diverse execution environments to
continue to exist and cooperate (i.e., coexistence rather than
assimilation).

