Strange.  I have 512MB DIMMS in a DS10.  But my story is not that good.
Bought two DS10's with Compaq memory and bought third party, supported
512MB DIMMS (DataRam).  They worked in one DS10, but not the other.  The 
mother board with the bad address line was replaced and then both 
worked with 512MB DIMMS, but somehow, ended up with both 256MB and 512MB 
DIMMS mixed in both, which worked fine for a year.  Then decided to put 
just 512MB DIMMS in the live system and 256MB DIMMS in the test system.  
About a week later, the live system started logging tons or correctable
memory errors on two DIMMS in different banks.  I removed those two and
put two 256MB DIMMS in (moving them around to match banks).  About a
week later, the other 512MB DIMMS gave errors.  Haven't had time to 
test the DIMMS and try them again in the other DS-10.

By the way, I may be misleading you with my size terminology.  When you
buy a 512MB upgrade, you actually get two 256MB DIMMS.  So it is only
512MB when you consider that they have to be matched pairs.  What I
called 512MB, is actually two 256MB DIMMS.  The real question is how
many address lines the XP1000 has.  That determines the max size DIMM
it will use.

Ray

