Compaq-HP-Intel: Inquiries continue

Dates too close for coincidence

By Mike Magee, 05/09/2001 10:22:10 BST

IT'S THE 12th of June, and Mr Michael "Curly" Capellas sits down in
Qastle Qapellas with his quill pen, to write a very long letter to his
staff, telling them he's given himself 180 days to turn the company
round.

His action plan includes the following:

* Create a dedicated solutions organization upstream
* Create four vertical solutions groups within the next three months
and four more within six to nine months
* Determine and deepen our go-to-market partnerships
* Change the metrics of revenue and margin reward systems without
losing accountability
* Retrain key elements of the sales force
* Create matching vertical practices in our service organization
downstream

It's the 22nd of June. The INQUIRER begins to get the whiff that Intel
will acquire Compaq's Alpha technology. A few days later, we're proved
right.

25th of June. Curly explains the Intel move to his staff, and tells
them the plan is backed by major customers including the British Stock
Exchange. This is a very important memo. It talks in detail about how
Compaq will continue to support its customers and its plans to carry
on with Open VMS.

Curly Capellas says: "Most important of all, we're creating great
value for our customers. They're getting the best of both worlds:
advanced systems engineering expertise from Compaq and leading
microprocessor design and world-class volume manufacturing from
Intel."

We jump to yesterday. The Wall Street Journal reports that Capellas
and HP's Fiorina started talking about "cross licensing" products in
June, although which date in June we don't know.

In July, she and Capellas start talking about a merger, only a few
days or weeks after he has written his now, obviously meaningless "180
day" memo, and is also obviously willing to abandon his customers -
including the Stock Exchange and many others which backed his plans on
the Itanium -- to the tender mercy of HP's sales team.

The INQUIRER was mercifully in the land of nod when the announcement
was made yesterday. But when we looked at our emails, we were tipped
off by someone who told us - before the official announcement - that
he'd been told by an Intel source that the deal was about to happen.

Help us with our Inquiries
We have some questions, some inquiries to pursue, in the 95 days in
between Mike Capellas' "180 day memo" and the beginning of the
"merger" discussions - and they relate to Intel, HP and Compaq.

Intel is desperate to push its Itanium 64-bit platform into the
corporate space, and co-developed the chip with HP's aid. Because only
a matter of days separated the ceding of Alpha technology to Intel and
the beginning of merger discussions between Compaq and HP, this raises
some highly uncomfortable questions - questions we hope that
customers, shareholders and regulatory authorities will not neglect to
ask.

On the 24th of February 1999, at an Intel Developer Forum, we reported
what general manager Paul Otellini had to say about Intel's business.
(Other Plaice). He said that Alpha is a spot where Intel had a gap and
where his company wanted to be.

During the Q-La conference call, where Otellini, Intel's Craig
Barrett, and Michael Capellas shared a platform, we were told that the
FTC would not have an interest in the ceding of the technology,
because the deal would "benefit the consumer".

It is stretching credulity to believe that the deal between Intel and
Compaq, then the deal between Compaq and HP, are unconnected.

IBM, Dell stand alone
What this deal has done is to put IBM in a position where it has been
effectively isolated with its Power family of processors, and where
the HP/Q "merger" means that Intel has a "preferential" position in
that deal.

