By combining the HP and Compaq PC business, and making huge cost cuts,
it is likely that the PC business sector will break even.  As you know
Compaq is a lot more than a PC builder.  Compaq have great technology,
both hardware and software, especially at the enterprise level.
Unfortunately the Compaq name has continued to be much more associated
with PCs and Proliant servers, than Alphaservers and Himalaya servers.
Also, until recently, it seemed that Compaq didn't know what to do with
it's enterprise technology and services.  Hopefully under HP, the
enterprise business will have a better stewardship.
Also, remember that HP had a big hand in the development of IA64.
Basically they bet their high end server business on this.  With the
Compaq/Intel deal, ex Alpha engineers will have a big say in the future
development of IA64.  Future IA64 processors should have all the
features HP and Compaq want.  A combined HP and Compaq should enable
IA64 to make big inroads into Sun SPARC and IBM Power4 server sales.  In
the hardware business, server sales, especially at the high end, is were
good margins are still being made.

So this is my take on things:
PCs and Wintel servers - consolidation of models.  Will become
profitable again.  This should provide good DELL competition.
UNIX - I can't see a future for separate HP-UX and Tru64 operating
systems.  I guess they will merge over time into one OS.  How this fits
in with the port of Tru64 from Alpha to IA64 is interesting.  The fact
that UX already runs on IA64 may be an indicator of a future strategy;
add Tru64 capabilities/compatibility on top of UX?  Sun should be
nervous about this.
OpenVMS - I guess that the IA64 port will still go ahead.  I cannot see
OpenVMS being actively developed in the future though.
Himalaya - HP don't have an equivalent product.  I think this will be
the least affected business unit.  Hopefully, HP will accelerate the
resurgence of this platform.  Himalaya can take business away from Sun
and IBM at the high end.  Just look at the recent Sabre deal.






